            "The first nurse researcher was the first modern nurse. In the British army hospital at Scutari during the Crimean War (1854–56) <strong>Florence Nightingale</strong> did not merely nurse; she counted. She recorded every admission and every death and its cause, and showed with her polar-area diagrams — the ‘coxcombs’ — that far more soldiers were dying of preventable infectious disease in the hospitals than of wounds on the battlefield, that the deaths fell when sanitation, ventilation and nutrition were improved, and that the same was true of the barracks in peacetime. She presented the numbers to politicians who could not ignore a picture, became in 1858 the first woman elected a Fellow of the Royal Statistical Society, and in <em>Notes on Nursing</em> (1859) founded a profession on observation rather than on sentiment. The lesson she left is the lesson of this course: a nurse who counts carefully and presents honestly can change a system. Nursing research grew slowly through the twentieth century — the first journal devoted to it, <em>Nursing Research</em>, appeared in 1952 — and moved from studying nurses (their education, their numbers) to studying what nurses do to patients and what patients experience, which is where it belongs.",
            "Ghana has a research tradition of its own that every nurse should be able to name. The <strong>Noguchi Memorial Institute for Medical Research</strong> at Legon, opened in 1979, is the national reference centre for the laboratory science behind malaria, HIV, tuberculosis and the haemorrhagic fevers. The Ghana Health Service's three field research centres have shaped national policy more than any ministry circular: <strong>Navrongo</strong> in the Upper East Region, where the Community Health and Family Planning Project begun in 1994 tested whether placing a trained nurse in the community with volunteer support would reduce child deaths and fertility — it did, and the result became national <strong>CHPS</strong> policy in 1999, which is why a community health officer now lives in your zone; <strong>Kintampo</strong> in the Bono East Region, which ran among the largest trials ever conducted in West Africa, including the ObaapaVitA trial of weekly vitamin A for women of childbearing age, which followed more than 200,000 women and found that the supplement did <em>not</em> reduce maternal deaths — a negative result that stopped a planned worldwide policy and saved resources for interventions that work — and the newborn home-visit studies that informed Ghana's community newborn care; and <strong>Dodowa</strong> in the Greater Accra Region, which studied health insurance, health-seeking behaviour and the health system itself. The RTS,S malaria vaccine was piloted in Ghana, Kenya and Malawi from 2019, with Ghanaian nurses giving the doses and Ghanaian researchers measuring the effect, before the World Health Organization recommended it for African children in 2021 and Ghana added it to the routine schedule. Seasonal malaria chemoprevention, intermittent preventive treatment in pregnancy, zinc with oral rehydration solution, kangaroo mother care and chlorhexidine cord care all reached the Ghanaian schedule by the same road: a question, a study, a result, a policy. The university schools of nursing and midwifery at Legon, Kumasi, Cape Coast, Ho and Tamale, the Ghana College of Nurses and Midwives and the Research and Development Division of the Ghana Health Service now train and support nurses to walk that road themselves.",
