            "Research begins with a <strong>problem</strong> — a gap between what is and what should be, or between what is known and what needs to be known — and the problems lie around every Ghanaian nurse in plain sight: the mother who stops exclusive breastfeeding when she returns to the market at six weeks, the man with hypertension who comes back in heart failure because the tablets were ‘finished’, the nurses who leave the northern district within a year of posting, the pressure ulcers that appear on the orthopaedic ward every rainy season, the partographs started late, the children with severe malaria who arrive after three days at a drug store. A problem becomes <strong>researchable</strong> when it can be answered by collecting data: ‘why do mothers stop exclusive breastfeeding at six weeks in Ho?’ is researchable; ‘the ward has no water’ is a problem for management, not for a study; ‘is breastfeeding good for babies?’ was answered long ago and needs a literature review, not a project; and ‘does God heal?’ cannot be answered by data at all. The sources of problems are clinical experience (the pattern you keep seeing), the literature (the gap the last study admitted), the national priorities (the Ghana Health Service's research agenda, the maternal and neonatal mortality targets, antimicrobial resistance), the audit that found a gap and could not explain it, and the patients who keep asking the same question. A good problem is <strong>significant</strong> (it matters to patients, to nurses or to the system), <strong>feasible</strong> (it can be done with the time, money, access, skills and participants available to this researcher — a final-year student cannot run a trial across three regions), <strong>ethical</strong> (it can be done without harm and with consent) and <strong>interesting to the researcher</strong>, who will live with it for a year.",
