            "No question is asked for the first time. The <strong>literature review</strong> is the systematic search for, reading of and account of what has already been found about the problem, and it serves five purposes: it tells the researcher whether the question has been answered (and so whether the study is needed, or should ask something narrower); it shows how others designed their studies, measured their variables and analysed their data, so that the new study can use validated tools and comparable methods; it identifies the gap the new study will fill and the controversy it may settle; it supplies the theoretical framework — the concepts and the model that organise the question; and it gives the proposal its credibility, because an examiner who sees that the student has read the twelve studies that matter believes the thirteenth will be done properly. A review is not a list of summaries (‘Mensah (2019) found… Owusu (2021) found…’) but an argument organised by theme, moving from the broad problem to the specific gap, comparing and criticising the studies as it goes, and ending with the sentence that justifies the present study.",
            "The <strong>search</strong> is planned, not browsed. The researcher writes her key terms and their synonyms — ‘exclusive breastfeeding’, ‘infant feeding’, ‘cessation’, ‘working mothers’, ‘Ghana’, ‘sub-Saharan Africa’ — combines them with AND and OR, and searches PubMed/MEDLINE, CINAHL (the nursing database), Google Scholar, the Cochrane Library for systematic reviews, African Journals Online, the Ghana Medical Journal and the university repositories where Ghanaian theses sit; HINARI through Research4Life gives free full-text access to most journals from Ghanaian institutions, and the <strong>grey literature</strong> — Ghana Health Service reports, the Demographic and Health Survey, World Health Organization guidelines, dissertations and conference abstracts — is searched too, because studies with negative or local results are published there when the journals decline them. Sources are weighed, not merely counted: a systematic review or a well-conducted trial carries more weight than a single cross-sectional survey, a recent study more than an old one on a changing subject, a peer-reviewed article more than a blog, and a study from a setting like ours more than one from a Scandinavian intensive care unit; the <strong>primary source</strong> (the study itself) is read and cited rather than someone else's summary of it, and an <strong>abstract alone</strong> is never enough to judge a study. Every source read is recorded at once — author, year, title, journal, volume, pages, the database, the date found and the key findings in the reader's own words — because the reference that cannot be found again in October was read in March.",
