          alt: "From a vague worry to a searchable question — a question you cannot search is a question you cannot answer; break it into four parts. The worry as it first arrives: ‘I think our patients get too many pressure sores.’ Population — who exactly? Adults immobile for more than 48 hours on a medical ward. Intervention — what would you do? Two-hourly repositioning on a written schedule. Comparison — compared with what? Repositioning when staff judge it necessary. Outcome — measured how? New pressure ulcers of grade 2 or worse in 14 days. The question you can now search and answer: in adults immobile beyond 48 hours, does scheduled two-hourly repositioning, compared with repositioning at staff discretion, reduce new grade 2 pressure ulcers within 14 days? The four parts also write the study: P becomes your inclusion criteria; I and C become your two groups; O becomes the thing you measure, and therefore your sample size calculation. If the question is not about an intervention, qualitative work asks about experience and meaning, so there is no comparison group and no measured outcome — use population, phenomenon of interest and context instead.",
          caption: "Population, intervention, comparison, outcome — the four parts turn a worry into a question, and the question into a study: P is the inclusion criteria, I and C are the two groups, O is the measurement and the sample size. For a qualitative question the parts change to population, phenomenon of interest and context, because experience has no comparison group.",
