            "A <strong>hypothesis</strong> is a tentative, testable prediction of the relationship between two or more variables, and it is stated only when the study will test a relationship — a trial, a cohort or case-control study, a correlational study. A descriptive survey (‘what proportion…?’) and a qualitative study (‘how do mothers experience…?’) have research questions but no hypothesis, because they predict nothing; a student who writes a hypothesis for a descriptive study has misunderstood both. The <strong>null hypothesis</strong> (H₀) states that there is <em>no</em> relationship or difference — ‘there is no difference in healing time between ulcers dressed with the new dressing and ulcers dressed with the standard dressing’ — and it is the hypothesis the statistical test actually tests, because the data can show that ‘no difference’ is implausible but can never prove that a difference exists with certainty; the <strong>alternative</strong> or research hypothesis (H₁) states the relationship the researcher expects. An alternative hypothesis is <strong>non-directional</strong> when it predicts a difference without saying which way (‘healing time differs between the two dressings’) and <strong>directional</strong> when it predicts the direction (‘wards with fewer nurses per patient have a higher incidence of pressure ulcers’), which is justified only when the literature already points one way. The hypothesis names the independent and dependent variables and the population, is written before the data are collected, and is accepted or rejected by the test chosen at the design stage — lesson 6.1 explains how.",
            "The honest proposal ends its first chapter by admitting three things. <strong>Assumptions</strong> are the things taken as true without testing — that participants will answer the questionnaire truthfully, that the ward register is accurate, that the HemoCue is calibrated. <strong>Delimitations</strong> are the boundaries the researcher chose — one hospital, one region, mothers of infants under six months, nurses with more than one year's service — which define to whom the findings may apply. <strong>Limitations</strong> are the weaknesses the researcher could not remove — a convenience sample, self-reported behaviour, a single observer, a short follow-up — stated so that the reader can weigh the findings, and restated in the final discussion with their likely effect on the results. A student who writes ‘this study has no limitations’ has announced that she does not understand her study; one who writes that her findings from a single district hospital cannot be generalised to the whole country, and explains why, has shown that she does.",
